To find $A$ and $B$, we take the following equation and multiply both sides by the common denominator:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{(x+2)(x-2)}&=\dfrac{A}{x+2}+\dfrac{B}{x-2}
\\\\
\displaystyle {1}&={A(x-2)}+{B(x+2)}
\end{align}$

Substituting $x=-2$, we get $\blueD{A=-\dfrac14}$.

Substituting $x=2$, we get $\maroonD{B=\dfrac14}$.
